/> from The Odin Project curriculum. Primarily, three concepts were covered: animations, accessibility, and responsive design.
</p>

The animations chapter taught transforms, transitions, and keyframes. Funnily enough though, the most interesting thing I personally learned while doing this chapter is the _pixel pipeline_.

<p>
Simply put, the pixel pipeline is a process used by web browsers to convert HTML, CSS, and JavaScript into visual elements shown on the screen. This 
<Navigable 
  content={"article"}
  href={"https://web.dev/articles/rendering-performance"}
  external={true}
  row={3}
  idx={0}
  underlined={true}
  multi={false}
/> mentions the five areas of this pipeline: JavaScript, style, layout, paint, and composite.
</p>

<p>
Also, this 
<Navigable 
  content={"resource"}
  href={"https://csstriggers.com/"}
  external={true}
  row={4}
  idx={0}
  underlined={true}
  multi={false}
/> is quite useful to see how an animatable CSS property may affect others. It's good to see which properties are performant.
</p>

<p>
As for the accessibility chapter, concepts discussed were semantic HTML, accessible colors, keyboard navigation, ARIA, and auditing tools. I find this 
<Navigable 
  content={"video from W3"}
  href={"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3f31oufqFSM"}
  external={true}
  row={5}
  idx={0}
  underlined={true}
  multi={false}
/> to be a good and quick refresher on web accessibility.
</p>

<p>
For this chapter, the 
<Navigable 
  content={"auditing tools lesson"}
  href={"https://www.theodinproject.com/lessons/node-path-advanced-html-and-css-accessibility-auditing"}
